Z||000000||Fformula88||05-27-2000||10:42 AM||||Is there anything else I can try to fix this.  My Fiero has the infamous idle problem, and its getting worse.  I have a new EGR tube, EGR valve, had the vacumn lines checked and repaired.  I have also had the sensors looked at and supposedly they are working fine.  What else could this possibly be?  Its gotten so bad the car is nearly undriveable.  It stalls immediately when its started the first time.  After its running, it idles up and down over a 300 RPM range.  Then it stalls at stopsigns and lights, and when I start it, it stalls when I put it back into gear (AUTO).  After it gets warm, it isn't so bad.  It will still idle up and down, but not as much.  It also stops stalling.  Anybody with any other advice?????????????  No shop or mechanic can figure it out.  I have had it to a few places with no luck.  I hope someone out there has an idea.  Thanks!||||reg||11
Z||000001||fieroshop||05-27-2000||11:23 AM||||Sounds like a problem we just had. Finally managed to find the culprit and it was a bad coil wire and coil.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hokie||05-27-2000||12:59 PM||||I just solved a very similar problem with my 86SE V6 auto.  First, see if this sounds like your problem.  The car ran O-ka except at idle.  When I would stop at an intersection, the car would sometimes nearly die and then rev back and forth from ~600 - ~1200 rpm.  At other times the problem didn't seem to be so severe, it just couldn't seem to find the proper idle rpm.  For me, the problem was worst when I would shift the transmission from drive or reverse into park.  When I did this, the car would immmediately rev to ~1200 rpm, then nearly die.  Then the engine would "hunt" for the idle until I shut it off.  Anyway, the solution.  Just a note of interest, my car only has 62,000 miles on it so it is a relatively low mileage car.  I replaced the distributor cap, rotor, plugs, wires, and checked the TPS (throttle position sensor) to no avail.  Then I bought one of those auto xray diagnostic tools and hooked it up.  The only thing that seemed unusual was that the idle air control valve setting was not at all stable at idle as it should be.  At this point, I wasn't sure if this was the cause or the effect.  I removed the air intake tube from the engine and looked into the throttle body.  It was full of soot.  I removed the IAC valve also, and it had a lot of soot buildup on it.  I bought a can of trottle body and air intake cleaner and I used about 3/4 of a can.  The intake on my car was filthy.  While I was at it, I also replaced the IAC valve, but I probably could have just cleaned the old one.  NAPA and Autozone sell replacement IAC valves.  Autozone is about $25 and NAPA is about twice that but may be of higher quality.  This fixed my problem.  My car idles fine now.  <P>I hope this helps.  ||||reg||3
Z||000003||Old Lar||05-27-2000||08:32 PM||||Same problem.  Fix was clean the thottle body with the cleaner stuff.  (Mr Goodwrench did it for me at a modest charge)<P>Car runs like new.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||05-28-2000||10:04 AM||||Thanks for all the advice.  It isn't the ignition system, because I also thought it sounded like a coil/wire problem.  I replaced them and did a full tune up.  No help.  I also cleaned out the throttle body because my old Probe acted similarly, and that was the problem.  It didn't help a whole lot either.  So I am thinking it is the IAC.<P>I pulled it out yesterday and tried to clean it off, as was a suggestion I found in an old posting.  I cannot manually move the piston of the valve back into the unit.  I can pull it out some, but it doesn't want to go back in.  I would think it would be tough so the air in the TB doesn't push it around, but not that tough.  Its worth a shot at replacing anyway.  <P>Again, Thanks for all your advice!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||05-28-2000||11:25 AM||||I think the idle is supposed to "hunt" just a little bit.  Maybe a couple hundred RPMs?||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||05-28-2000||02:24 PM||||They aren't *supposed* to hunt, they just tend to hunt especially if the TB is dirty.<P>The normal idle IAC counts will be around 80 or so. Higher in DIAG mode. With the key on and engine stopped, it will be 90 or so.||||reg||12
